{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794412587,"nonce":"yeyKIykTpoiA+i2g+CBL4bqopnQ=","title":"The hxn command: what it does and what is still open","message":"Before this, using hxn meant three crates, two build scripts, a run of\n`hxn-gen` and a list of `haxe` flags, which is what `tests/counter` shows and\ntoo much for a readme. `crates/hxn-cli` builds the `hxn` command so that a\nlibrary is one crate.\n\n\n- `hxn new \u003cname\u003e [--hxn \u003ccheckout\u003e]`: a crate depending on `hxn`, an example\n  `Greeter` in `src/lib.rs`, and `haxe/Main.hx`. Without `--hxn` it depends on\n  the repository the command was built from.\n- `hxn build \u003chl|cpp\u003e [--release]`: parses the library with `hxn-gen`, writes\n  the manifest beside the crate and everything else under\n  `\u003ctarget\u003e/hxn/\u003clibrary\u003e/`, builds the adapter crate there with cargo, copies\n  the `.hdll` into place, and writes `build.hxml`.\n- `hxn run \u003chl|cpp\u003e [--vm \u003cprogram\u003e] [-- \u003carguments\u003e]`: builds, compiles\n  `haxe/` with main class `Main`, and runs it. For HashLink the VM is `--vm`,\n  `HXN_VM` or `hl`, and the library path variables are set so the loader\n  finds the `.hdll` and the VM's own library.\n- `[package.metadata.hxn]` in the library's `Cargo.toml`: `library`, `source`,\n  `haxe`, `main`.\n- The generated adapter crate names `hxn-abi` and `hxn-hl` from wherever\n  `cargo metadata` says the library's `hxn` comes from. `hxn-hl` now\n  re-exports `vbyte` and `vstring`, so a generated adapter needs no\n  dependency on `hl_abi` of its own.\n- The command embeds `haxe/hxn/*.hx` and `include/hxn.h`.\n\n\n`tests/cli/run.sh` on Ash and hxcpp (macOS) and stock HashLink 1.15 (the\nNUC). By hand: a library taking `hxn` as a git dependency from a scratch\nclone, and `cargo install --git` of the command from that clone.\n\n\n- Adapter crates kept in the library's repository, each with a build script,\n  as the fixture has them. Still possible; it is three crates to explain.\n- hxn's Haxe classes as a haxelib. One more thing to install and to keep at\n  the same version as the command; embedding makes the command enough.\n- A fixed git URL for the adapter's hxn crates. A library pinned to another\n  revision would then build against two copies of `hxn-abi`.\n\n\n- The command and the library's `hxn` can be different revisions. Nothing\n  checks that the adapter the command writes suits the `hxn-hl` it is built\n  against.\n- The adapter crate has its own lock file, seeded from the library's the\n  first time and not kept in step afterwards.\n- Not run with hxcpp on Linux: the NUC has no hxcpp installed. Not run on\n  Windows at all; the `.lib` and `.dll` names there are untested guesses.\n- No wasm or Emscripten runtime: `tests/counter/run.sh` still does those by\n  hand, and nothing generated runs there yet.\n- No way to pass a VM its own options (`ash --mode jit`), or `haxe` extra\n  flags (`-lib`, `-D`, `-debug`).\n- `build.hxml` does not quote paths; one with spaces is untested.\n- `include_str!` reaches outside the crate for the Haxe classes and the\n  header, which works from a checkout or a git install and would not from a\n  packaged crate on crates.io.\n- `hxn new` in a directory under another cargo workspace makes a crate cargo\n  refuses as a stray member.\n- No `hxn check`, to compare the committed manifest with the source without\n  building.","files":null}]}